emH(){var s,r,q,p,o,n,m="\t",l=t.s,k='Enclosure Ratings (IP and NEMA)\n\nThe two rating systems that tell you whether an enclosure, AP, antenna, or PoE injector will survive dust and water where you plan to mount it. IP comes from IEC 60529 (international). NEMA comes from NEMA 250 (US). US spec sheets quote NEMA, international sheets quote IP, and pros translate between them.\n\nIP code (IEC 60529)\nAn IP rating reads left to right: IP then two digits. First digit is solids and dust. Second digit is water. Each digit maps to a defined lab test, which is why the code is trustworthy and "waterproof" is not.\nIP 6 7 means 6 = dust-tight, 7 = survives temporary immersion.\n\nFirst digit: solids and dust\n'+(B.b.H(A.a(["Digit","Protection","Test gate"],l),m)+"\n")
for(s=0;s<7;++s){r=B.ZW[s]
k+=B.b.H(A.a([r.a,r.b,r.c],l),m)+"\n"}k=k+"The object-size ladder ends at 4 (1 mm). Levels 5 and 6 switch to a dust-chamber test, so they are about dust, not a bigger object.\n\nSecond digit: water\n"+(B.b.H(A.a(["Digit","Protection","Plain meaning"],l),m)+"\n")
for(s=0;s<10;++s){r=B.a1d[s]
k+=B.b.H(A.a([r.a,r.b,r.c],l),m)+"\n"}k+="Two levels get misread. 8 is manufacturer-defined, not a fixed depth. 9K is about pressure-washing, a different hazard than immersion.\n\nThe X placeholder and the letters\n"
for(s=0;s<3;++s)k+="- "+B.a0d[s]+"\n"
k=k+"\nCommon IP ratings you actually see\n"+(B.b.H(A.a(["Rating","Plain meaning","Typical WLAN example"],l),m)+"\n")
for(s=0;s<7;++s){q=B.a46[s]
k+=B.b.H(A.a([q.a,q.b,q.c],l),m)+"\n"}k=k+"\nNEMA (NEMA 250)\nNEMA types describe the same idea for US spec sheets, and they test things IP never checks: corrosion, external icing, gasket aging, and oil. The four types that matter for Wi-Fi:\n"+(B.b.H(A.a(["Type","Plain meaning"],l),m)+"\n")
for(s=0;s<4;++s){p=B.a1i[s]
k+=B.b.H(A.a([p.a,p.b],l),m)+"\n"}k=k+"The full type list also includes 1, 2, 3, 3S, 5, 12, and 13 (indoor and specialized).\n\nNEMA to IP: a one-way relationship\nA NEMA type maps to a minimum IP equivalent. The reverse is not valid. NEMA tests corrosion, icing, gasket aging, and oil, so a NEMA 4X enclosure exceeds IP66, but an IP66 enclosure is not automatically NEMA 4X. It was never corrosion-tested.\nNEMA to IP: valid as a minimum. IP to NEMA: not valid.\n"+(B.b.H(A.a(["NEMA type","Minimum IP (commonly cited)"],l),m)+"\n")
for(s=0;s<5;++s){o=B.Zd[s]
k+=B.b.H(A.a([o.a,o.b],l),m)+"\n"}k=k+"NEMA publishes no exact conversion, and reputable charts disagree on a couple of rows. Where they differ, both values are shown. Treat these as approximate floors, never as certified equivalences.\n\nWhat rating for what placement\n"+(B.b.H(A.a(["Placement","Reach for","Why"],l),m)+"\n")
for(s=0;s<5;++s){n=B.zZ[s]
k+=B.b.H(A.a([n.a,n.b,n.c],l),m)+"\n"}l=k+"\nMyths worth killing\n"
for(s=0;s<5;++s)l+="- "+B.a1l[s]+"\n"
l+="\nWhy a WLAN pro cares\n"
for(s=0;s<2;++s)l+=B.a2L[s]+"\n"
l+="\nThis is a field reference, not code or design guidance. Confirm requirements with the AHJ, the architect of record, and a licensed electrician.\n"
return B.f.ah(l.charCodeAt(0)==0?l:l)},
