for(s=0;s<2;++s){p=B.Zi[s]
m+=B.b.H(A.a([p.a,p.b,p.c],n),o)+"\n"}n=m+"\nPower factor (cos phi) is 1 for purely resistive loads and for DC, where P = V x I is exact. Power factor is less than 1 whenever the load is reactive (inductive motors and compressors, or switch-mode power supplies); there V x I gives apparent power in VA, not real power in watts. That gap is exactly why UPS and PDU equipment is rated in both VA and W. Do not read P = V x I as giving watts on an AC reactive load.\n\n"+"All four identities derive from V = I x R and P = V x I. For three-phase balanced loads the line-to-line voltage is the square root of 3 times the line-to-neutral voltage.\n"
