B.bsN=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/cover.svg",616,360,null,null)
B.bxa=new A.b3("A travel router is a small box you pack in your bag. It joins the hotel Wi-Fi for you. Then it makes your own Wi-Fi in the room. Your phone, laptop and tablet join that, just like at home.")
B.bHU=new A.aS("This guide answers three questions. What is a travel router? Why does it help you? And how do you set one up?",!1)
B.boG=new A.bC(B.ak,"A word on the words","A **router** is the box that shares one internet connection with many devices. A **travel router** is a pocket-sized one. A **sign-in page** is the web page a hotel shows before it lets you online.",null,null,B.r)
B.bpt=new A.bC(B.ak,"What this guide leaves out","Hotel sign-in pages have their own guide: __Captive Portals, Explained__. This guide covers only what changes when a travel router is in the room.",null,null,B.r)
B.Y7=new A.kB("captive-portals-explained",null,null)
B.cmy=s([B.bxa,B.bHU,B.boG,B.bpt,B.Y7],t.X)
B.bG9=new A.aE("1","The short answer",B.cmy,null)
B.bz0=new A.b3("A travel router talks to the hotel, and it talks to you. It does both at once.")
B.bt1=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-01.svg",616,300,"**Figure 1. Two jobs, one box.** It joins the hotel Wi-Fi, just like a phone would. Then it makes a Wi-Fi network that belongs to you.",null)
B.brT=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-02.svg",616,306,"**Figure 2. Your Wi-Fi is a few steps away.** The hotel's Wi-Fi often comes from down the hall. Your own Wi-Fi comes from the desk, right next to you.",null)
B.bYP=s([B.bz0,B.bt1,B.brT],t.X)
B.bFo=new A.aE("2","A small box with two jobs",B.bYP,null)
B.bzv=new A.b3("A better travel router has two radios, one for each job. One faces the hotel. The other faces you.")
B.btQ=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-03.svg",616,460,"**Figure 3. One radio for each job.** The top radio joins the hotel. The bottom radio runs your own Wi-Fi. In the middle, the box sends everything out as if it were one device.",null)
B.d2=new A.a4L(3,"quote")
B.bpa=new A.bC(B.d2,null,"\u201cA travel router is two radios wearing one name.\u201d","Keith's note",u.jC,B.r)
B.bQ9=s([B.bzv,B.btQ,B.bpa],t.X)
B.bFP=new A.aE("3","Two radios in one box",B.bQ9,null)
B.bzo=new A.b3("Your phone joins a network it already knows by itself. With a travel router, you have one network to know, in every hotel.")
B.brf=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-04.svg",616,352,"**Figure 4. Sign in once, not on every device.** Only the travel router meets each new hotel. Your devices see the same network every trip, so they join on their own.",null)
B.bvc=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-05.svg",616,240,"**Figure 5. Help for gadgets with no browser.** A TV stick or a game console can struggle with a sign-in page. On your own Wi-Fi, it never sees one.",null)
B.chw=s([B.bzo,B.brf,B.bvc],t.X)
B.bEN=new A.aE("4","Your devices remember one network",B.chw,null)
B.byM=new A.b3("Some hotels let each guest sign in only a few devices. A family can hit that limit before the bags are open.")
B.btI=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-06.svg",616,344,"**Figure 6. The hotel counts one.** Usually, every device behind the travel router shares that one spot. The limit shown is an example. If a hotel still blocks it, ask the front desk.",null)
B.brB=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-07.svg",616,246,"**Figure 7. One on the list, many in the room.** The hotel sees one device. Everything you carry sits behind it.",null)
B.c8i=s([B.byM,B.btI,B.brB],t.X)
B.bEb=new A.aE("5","The device limit",B.c8i,null)
B.bzl=new A.b3("Hotel Wi-Fi is shared with every guest in the building. A travel router puts a wall between your devices and that crowd.")
B.bst=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-08.svg",616,344,"**Figure 8. Your devices stay on your side.** On hotel Wi-Fi, your devices may share a network with strangers. Many hotels keep guests apart, but you can't tell which. Behind the travel router, your devices sit on your own network.",null)
B.bpf=new A.bC(B.ak,"Leave it in router mode","Some travel routers also offer an access point or bridge mode. Those modes take the wall down. Router mode keeps it up.",null,null,B.r)
B.bnV=new A.bC(B.ak,"It is still the hotel's internet","The travel router keeps other guests away from your devices. It does not hide what you do online from the hotel. For that, see the VPN note in step 12.",null,null,B.r)
B.bPT=s([B.bzl,B.bst,B.bpf,B.bnV],t.X)
B.bGF=new A.aE("6","A network of your own",B.bPT,null)
B.bxt=new A.b3("Do the setup at home, before the trip, where you have time and good internet.")
B.btu=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-09.svg",616,240,"**Figure 9. Three steps, done once.** Give your network a name and a password, then join each device to it. After that, they join by themselves.",null)
B.c_U=s(["**Plug it in and join its Wi-Fi.** The name and password are on its label.","**Open its settings.** The box or the manual tells you how. Many use an app.","**Change the name and password to your own.** Set a password for the settings page too.","**Join every device you travel with.** Phones, laptops, tablets, the TV stick.","**Update its software** while you have good internet."],t.s)
B.bH7=new A.hZ(B.c_U)
B.brr=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-10.svg",616,180,"**Figure 10. Three things to pack.** The travel router, its power cord, and a short network cable in case the room has a wall jack.",null)
B.bSV=s([B.bxt,B.btu,B.bH7,B.brr],t.X)
B.bF4=new A.aE("7","Set it up at home",B.bSV,null)
B.bzB=new A.b3("In the room, you tell the travel router which hotel network to join. You do it from your phone.")
B.buq=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-11.svg",616,240,"**Figure 11. Pick the hotel network.** On your phone, open the travel router's settings. Choose the hotel's network from the list, and the router joins it.",null)
B.bsE=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-12.svg",616,286,"**Figure 12. One sign-in covers everyone.** Open a web page on a phone joined to your travel router. The hotel's page shows up. Accept it once, and every device is online.",null)
B.bHK=new A.aS("No page? On the router, turn off any VPN or ad blocker for a moment. Then open a web page in your browser. __Captive Portals, Explained__ has more help.",!1)
B.cbU=s([B.bzB,B.buq,B.bsE,B.bHK,B.Y7],t.X)
B.bGk=new A.aE("8","At the hotel: join, then sign in",B.cbU,null)
B.bwZ=new A.b3("Some rooms still have a network jack in the wall or on the desk. If yours does, use it.")
B.bt9=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-13.svg",616,344,"**Figure 13. A cable is steadier.** The cable carries the hotel side. The travel router's Wi-Fi then works only for you and your devices.",null)
B.bsv=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-14.svg",616,230,"**Figure 14. Use the internet port.** The cable from the wall goes into the port meant for the internet. The jack may show the same sign-in page as the Wi-Fi.",null)
B.cmm=s([B.bwZ,B.bt9,B.bsv],t.X)
B.bEC=new A.aE("9","A wired jack in the room",B.cmm,null)
B.bs6=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-15.svg",616,200,"**Figure 15. Slow in, slow out.** The travel router shares what the hotel gives. It can make things steadier, but not faster.",null)
B.boA=new A.bC(B.d2,null,"\u201cA travel router does not make a bad network good. It changes which device has to cope with the bad network, and it changes what everything else can see.\u201d","Keith's note",u.jC,B.r)
B.bvH=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-16.svg",616,290,"**Figure 16. Check for two radios.** A one-radio model takes turns talking to the hotel and to you, so it runs slower.",null)
B.cac=s([B.bs6,B.boA,B.bvH],t.X)
B.bDX=new A.aE("10","When it doesn't help",B.cac,null)
B.bzA=new A.b3("Your devices like your travel router so much that they stick to it. Most days, that's what you want.")
B.brj=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-17.svg",616,318,"**Figure 17. Known beats nearby.** Your devices usually pick the network they know. To put one on the hotel Wi-Fi, turn the travel router off, or tell that device to forget your network.",null)
B.bs9=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-18.svg",616,250,"**Figure 18. The wall works both ways.** Other guests can't reach your devices. Your devices also may not find things on the hotel network, like a room TV you cast video to, because they are on a different network.",null)
B.bRG=s([B.bzA,B.brj,B.bs9],t.X)
B.bGf=new A.aE("11","Two surprises",B.bRG,null)
B.bzq=new A.b3("A VPN (virtual private network) on the travel router scrambles what your devices send, so the hotel can't read it, and you trust the VPN company instead.")
B.bu8=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-19.svg",616,180,"**Figure 19. One tunnel for every device.** Set up on the router, the VPN covers everything behind it.",null)
B.ckw=s([B.bzq,B.bu8],t.X)
B.bE6=new A.aE("12","A VPN on the router",B.ckw,null)
B.bBg=new A.ce("A travel router makes hotel Wi-Fi faster.","It shares the same hotel internet. It can feel better because it's steady and close to you, but it can't add speed.")
B.bA6=new A.ce("A travel router brings its own internet.","The kind in this guide has none of its own. It shares the hotel's, by Wi-Fi or by cable.")
B.bBb=new A.ce("Behind a travel router, the hotel can't see what I do.","It keeps other guests away from your devices. The hotel still carries your traffic. A VPN is what hides it.")
B.bA9=new A.ce("You have to be a tech expert.","You set it up once, at home. At each hotel, you pick a network and accept a sign-in page.")
B.c03=s([B.bBg,B.bA6,B.bBb,B.bA9],t.X)
B.bER=new A.aE("13","Four things people get wrong",B.c03,null)
B.bu4=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/travel-routers/fig-20.svg",616,430,"**Figure 20. Two easy wins.** Set it up before you leave, and keep it where your devices can hear it.",null)
B.clE=s(["**Set it up at home.** Do it before the trip, not late at night in a hotel.","**Pack the cable and the power cord.** A short network cable is worth the space.","**Put it in the open, near where you sit.** Not in a bag or a drawer.","**Sign in from a device on your travel router.** Then the sign-in covers everyone.","**Turn it off when you want a device on another network.**","**Buy a model with two radios.** Check the box or the maker's page."],t.s)
B.bGJ=new A.hZ(B.clE)
B.c7m=s([B.bu4,B.bGJ],t.X)
B.bEL=new A.aE("14","Tips",B.c7m,null)
B.bIq=new A.aS("Checked on 28 September 2026. Vendor pages are cited as sources only, not as recommendations.",!1)
B.clN=s(['GL.iNet Router Docs 4, "Repeater" (docs.gl-inet.com/router/en/4/interface_guide/internet_repeater/): the router creates its own subnet and acts as a firewall between you and the public network','GL.iNet Router Docs 4, "Connect to public hotspot with Captive Portal" (docs.gl-inet.com/router/en/4/faq/connect_to_a_hotspot_with_captive_portal/): turn off VPN and DNS rebinding protection before the sign-in page; MAC clone for device-tied sign-ins','GL.iNet Router Docs 4, "Ethernet" (docs.gl-inet.com/router/en/4/interface_guide/internet_ethernet/): connect the router\'s internet port to an Ethernet jack','GL.iNet blog, "Bypass Captive Portals and Device Limits," 17 October 2024: some hotels limit devices; only the router signs in; a VPN on the router covers every connected device','GL.iNet Router Docs 4, "Network Mode" (docs.gl-inet.com/router/en/4/interface_guide/network_mode/): access point mode turns off NAT and DHCP; router mode keeps NAT, DHCP and the firewall','Google for Developers, Cast "Discovery Troubleshooting": the sender and the Cast device must be on the same Wi-Fi network','Apple Support 102169, "How Apple devices decide which known wireless networks to auto-join"','US Federal Trade Commission, "In the market for a VPN app?," February 2018','IETF RFC 3022, "Traditional IP Network Address Translator," January 2001: many devices sharing one address',"Keith Parsons, LinkedIn post on travel routers, 3 September 2026"],t.s)
B.bCj=new A.js(B.clN)
B.bnQ=new A.bC(B.ak,"About this guide","Written for people who aren't technical and want their devices online in a hotel with less fuss. It is independent, and isn't affiliated with or endorsed by any router maker or hotel company. It names no brand to buy.",null,null,B.r)
B.fM=new A.bC(B.ak,"About the author","Keith Parsons, CWNE #3, has designed, installed and troubleshot Wi-Fi for over 25 years. He runs Wireless LAN Professionals and the WLPC conferences.",null,null,B.r)
