B.buZ=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/wifi-and-health/cover.svg",760,260,null,null)
B.by9=new A.b3("A Wi-Fi router sends about a tenth of a watt. That's tiny. The signal also fades fast as it spreads out. In real homes and schools, it measures far below the safety limits.")
B.bqn=new A.fo("The power","A router sends about a tenth of a watt. A microwave oven uses thousands of times more, and keeps it inside a metal box.")
B.bq7=new A.fo("The distance","Radio power thins out as it spreads. Step twice as far away and you get a quarter as much.")
B.bpQ=new A.fo("The agencies","Health agencies have looked hard. They have found no consistent evidence that Wi-Fi within the limits harms people.")
B.cax=s([B.bqn,B.bq7,B.bpQ],t.UA)
B.bqt=new A.m7(B.cax)
B.bnL=new A.bC(B.ak,"About this guide","This guide explains the power numbers in plain words. It isn't medical advice. If you have a question about your own health, ask your doctor.",null,null,B.r)
B.bpd=new A.bC(B.ak,"A word on the words","A **watt** measures power. **Radiation** just means energy that travels outward. Light and radio waves count, and so do X-rays. But they are different kinds, as the next step shows.",null,null,B.r)
B.bRm=s([B.by9,B.bqt,B.bnL,B.bpd],t.X)
B.bEm=new A.aE("1","The short answer",B.bRm,null)
B.bz_=new A.b3("The word radiation covers everything from radio to X-rays. What matters is which kind.")
B.bI7=new A.aS("Scientists split it into two groups. X-rays carry enough energy to knock bits off atoms. That's called **ionizing** radiation. Radio waves and light don't have that much energy. They're called **non-ionizing**. Wi-Fi is on the non-ionizing side.",!1)
B.bu9=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/wifi-and-health/fig-01.svg",760,420,"**Figure 1. The range of energy, from radio to X-rays.** Energy rises from left to right. Wi-Fi sits in the microwave band. The dividing line falls in the ultraviolet, and too much of that burns skin. Not to scale.",null)
B.bJc=new A.aS("What radio waves can do, if the power is high enough, is heat things. That's how a microwave oven cooks. The safety limits are built around heating (step 6).",!1)
B.bo2=new A.bC(B.d2,null,"\u201cWi-Fi is low-power, non-ionizing radio energy.\u201d","Keith's note","Keith Parsons, LinkedIn post, 2026",B.r)
B.c1S=s([B.bz_,B.bI7,B.bu9,B.bJc,B.bo2],t.X)
B.bDv=new A.aE("2","Radio waves are not X-rays",B.c1S,null)
B.bya=new A.b3("A typical Wi-Fi router sends about a tenth of a watt. Your phone or laptop sends about the same over Wi-Fi.")
B.br3=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/wifi-and-health/fig-02.svg",760,395.75,"**Figure 2. The same four powers, drawn two ways.** (a) On an ordinary ruler the oven fills the width. The router, phone and Wi-Fi cap are too thin to see. (b) Each step to the right is ten times the one before. On this scale the oven looks close. On a ruler it's thousands of times the router. Every bar ends at its value.",null)
B.bJS=new A.aS("__Scales and Ratios, Explained__ shows how these power numbers work.",!1)
B.Y5=new A.kB("scales-and-ratios-explained",null,null)
B.bzm=new A.b3("A kitchen microwave oven uses about 1,200 watts. That's thousands of times more than a router. And the oven's metal box keeps the energy inside.")
B.bt6=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/wifi-and-health/fig-03.svg",760,500,"**Figure 3. Thousands of times the power, kept in a box.** Left, the oven's energy bounces around inside its metal box. Right, a router's tenth of a watt spreads out into the room. Not to scale.",null)
B.cjQ=s([B.bya,B.br3,B.bJS,B.Y5,B.bzm,B.bt6],t.X)
B.bDY=new A.aE("3","A tenth of a watt",B.cjQ,null)
B.byz=new A.b3("A router's signal spreads out as it travels. The same power has to cover a bigger and bigger area.")
B.bqZ=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/wifi-and-health/fig-04.svg",760,384,"**Figure 4. The same power, spread over more area.** Twice as far away, the power covers four squares. So each square gets a quarter. Not to scale.",null)
B.bL2=new A.aS("Scientists call this the inverse-square law. Step twice as far away, and you get a quarter of the power. Step four times as far, and you get a sixteenth.",!1)
B.bxd=new A.b3("Walls and furniture soak up some of the signal too. So in a real home, it fades even faster.")
B.brx=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/wifi-and-health/fig-05.svg",760,330,"**Figure 5. Walls soak up signal too.** Top, open air. Bottom, the same distance through a wall: less reaches the far side. Not to scale.",null)
B.bnA=new A.bC(B.d2,null,"\u201cWalls, floors, furniture, and distance all chew up the radio signal between you and the box on the wall, and the further away you sit, the more gets chewed up.\u201d","Keith's note",u.h,B.r)
B.c2z=s([B.byz,B.bqZ,B.bL2,B.bxd,B.brx,B.bnA],t.X)
B.bGy=new A.aE("4","Why distance matters so much",B.c2z,null)
B.bxQ=new A.b3("Now compare a router with the safety limit for the public.")
B.bHV=new A.aS("Picture a router sending nonstop, with nothing in the way. At 1 m (3 feet), it reaches about a thousandth of the limit.",!1)
B.bub=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/wifi-and-health/fig-06.svg",760,224,"**Figure 6. How much of the public limit a router reaches.** A router sending nonstop, with no walls in the way. Distances to scale.",null)
B.bJ0=new A.aS("Real routers send in short bursts, only when there's data to move. So real levels are lower still.",!1)
B.bu_=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/wifi-and-health/fig-07.svg",760,244,"**Figure 7. A real router sends in short bursts.** Top, the math in this step. Bottom, a real router, sending only when there's data to move. Not to scale.",null)
B.bJB=new A.aS("Even the strongest router the rules allow stays well under the limit at arm's length.",!1)
B.bRd=s([B.bxQ,B.bHV,B.bub,B.bJ0,B.bu_,B.bJB],t.X)
B.bDJ=new A.aE("5","How far below the limit?",B.bRd,null)
B.bwV=new A.b3("The limits start with the level of radio energy that warms the body. Then they add a big safety margin.")
B.bKY=new A.aS("Scientists found the level that warms the whole body by about 1 \xb0C (2 \xb0F).",!1)
B.bK6=new A.aS("For workers who know about the exposure, the limit is a tenth of that level. For everyone else, it's a fiftieth.",!1)
B.bsC=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/wifi-and-health/fig-08.svg",760,184,"**Figure 8. The safety margin.** The public limit is a fiftieth of the level that warms the body. Each bar is drawn to scale.",null)
B.bKm=new A.aS("The limit also assumes you're in the signal all day, every day, with no end.",!1)
B.bKj=new A.aS("Phones held to your head are a different question from Wi-Fi. They get their own test, and this guide doesn't cover them.",!1)
B.bNB=s([B.bwV,B.bKY,B.bK6,B.bsC,B.bKm,B.bKj],t.X)
B.bFv=new A.aE("6","How the limits are set",B.bNB,null)
B.bym=new A.b3("Math shows the most a router could reach. Australia's radiation agency went into schools to measure what was there.")
B.bJe=new A.aS("They measured Wi-Fi in 23 schools. In a typical classroom, Wi-Fi was about a millionth of the limit. That's far below the math in step 5.",!1)
B.brL=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/wifi-and-health/fig-09.svg",760,507.75,'**Figure 9. Figured and measured, against the public limit.** (a) On an ordinary ruler, only the limit shows. (b) Each step to the right is ten times the one before. Classroom values are rounded, so they are shown as "about."',null)
B.bKq=new A.aS("The UK's health agency tested schools too. It found Wi-Fi exposure was small compared with the limits.",!1)
B.bwg=new A.ew("What the agencies say")
B.bov=new A.bC(B.d2,null,"\u201cThere is no consistent evidence that exposure to radio signals from Wi-Fi devices adversely affects the health of the general population.\u201d","UK Health Security Agency","UKHSA, Wi-Fi radio waves and health, updated 19 February 2025",B.r)
B.bpz=new A.bC(B.d2,null,"\u201cTo date, and after much research performed, no adverse health effect has been causally linked with exposure to wireless technologies.\u201d","World Health Organization","WHO, Radiation: 5G mobile networks and health, 27 February 2020",B.r)
B.bHM=new A.aS("In plain words: after a lot of research, they have not found that Wi-Fi within the limits harms people. They keep watching new studies.",!1)
B.bOP=s([B.bym,B.bJe,B.brL,B.bKq,B.bwg,B.bov,B.bpz,B.bHM],t.X)
B.bFi=new A.aE("7","What was measured, and what agencies say",B.bOP,null)
B.bwR=new A.b3("Each of these comes up often. Each one has a simple answer.")
B.bAg=new A.ce("A router puts out about as much power as a microwave oven.","The typical oven uses 1,200 W. A router uses thousands of times less. And the oven's metal box keeps the energy inside.")
B.bBi=new A.ce("Turning Wi-Fi off at night makes a big difference.","Across a room, a router reaches a tiny sliver of the limit. Health agencies say no extra steps are needed. Turning it off is still your choice (step 9).")
B.bA_=new A.ce("Newer Wi-Fi raised the power.","Power rules are set for each band of radio frequencies, not for each version of Wi-Fi. A new version doesn't get a bigger allowance.")
B.bBf=new A.ce("Nobody has studied it.","Health agencies have studied it a lot. Some even measured Wi-Fi in schools (step 7).")
B.bYu=s([B.bwR,B.bAg,B.bBi,B.bA_,B.bBf],t.X)
B.bEd=new A.aE("8","Four things people get wrong",B.bYu,null)
B.byW=new A.b3("Health agencies say no extra steps are needed. Some people want to take steps anyway. For Wi-Fi, distance does almost all the work.")
B.bve=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/wifi-and-health/fig-10.svg",760,400,"**Figure 10. A shelf across the room.** The same router, moved from beside the sofa to a shelf on the far wall.",null)
B.bqr=new A.fo("Keep a little distance from the router.","Four times farther away means a sixteenth of the power.")
B.bq_=new A.fo("Put the router where nobody sits right beside it for hours.","A shelf across the room does more than any gadget.")
B.bpS=new A.fo("Turning Wi-Fi off at night is your choice.","It isn't needed. If you do it, know what stops working.")
