B.btC=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/analog-vs-digital/cover.svg",700,240,null,null)
B.byj=new A.b3("Analog keeps a smooth copy of something. Digital measures it again and again, and keeps only the numbers.")
B.bL3=new A.aS("This guide answers three questions. What do the two words mean? Why does it matter to you? And what can you do when a picture freezes or Wi-Fi crawls?",!1)
B.boN=new A.bC(B.ak,"The one idea behind this guide","Noise makes an analog signal worse a little at a time. A digital signal ignores noise until it can't, and then it fails all at once. Wi-Fi sits in between. It slows down in steps to stay connected.",null,null,B.r)
B.bpg=new A.bC(B.ak,"A word on the words","A **signal** is anything that carries information, like a sound or a radio wave. A **bit** is a single 1 or 0, the smallest piece of digital data.",null,null,B.r)
B.cfx=s([B.byj,B.bL3,B.boN,B.bpg],t.X)
B.bEO=new A.aE("1","The short answer",B.cfx,null)
B.bxL=new A.b3("Put a record on a turntable. The needle rides a groove cut in the shape of the sound. That's analog.")
B.bHG=new A.aS("Now stream the same song. Your phone gets a file of numbers. Someone measured the sound over and over, fast, and wrote down each measurement. That's digital.",!1)
B.btF=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/analog-vs-digital/fig-01.svg",784,328,"**Figure 1. A groove or a list of numbers.** A record keeps the shape of the sound. A phone keeps measurements of it.",null)
B.bts=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/analog-vs-digital/fig-02.svg",760,300,"**Figure 2. The same sound, kept two ways.** The analog copy follows the wave everywhere. The digital copy keeps only the dots, and stores each dot as a number.",null)
B.bK7=new A.aS("A CD measures the sound 44,100 times every second. That's fast enough that your ears can't tell the difference.",!1)
B.buG=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/analog-vs-digital/fig-03.svg",784,714,"**Figure 3. More measurements make a closer copy.** Join the dots and you get the digital copy. With enough dots, it lands right on the real sound.",null)
B.ck6=s([B.bxL,B.bHG,B.btF,B.bts,B.bK7,B.buG],t.X)
B.bE9=new A.aE("2","A smooth copy or a list of numbers",B.ck6,null)
B.bzu=new A.b3("Copy a cassette tape, then copy the copy. Each time, you get a little more hiss. Analog copies wear out.")
B.bJC=new A.aS("A digital copy is just the same list of numbers. The computer checks every number, so the copy is exact. Your hundredth copy of a photo is as good as the first.",!1)
B.btr=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/analog-vs-digital/fig-04.svg",784,470,"**Figure 4. Copies of copies.** Every tape copy adds a little hiss, but every digital copy is the same list of numbers. Read each row from left to right.",null)
B.bKf=new A.aS("Your ears are still analog, and so are your speakers. So your phone turns the numbers back into a smooth wave before you hear it. It doesn't come out as a staircase.",!1)
B.brN=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/analog-vs-digital/fig-05.svg",784,442,"**Figure 5. Analog at both ends, digital in the middle.** Sound is only numbers while it is stored or sent. Before the speaker plays it, the phone smooths the steps back into a wave.",null)
B.bnX=new A.bC(B.ak,"Files don't fade, but drives fail","A digital file never wears out, but the phone or drive holding it can break. Keep two copies of anything you can't replace, in two different places.",null,null,B.r)
B.bta=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/analog-vs-digital/fig-06.svg",784,208,"**Figure 6. One copy can be lost. Two copies in two places are safer.** A cloud backup counts as a second place, but a delete on your phone can delete it there too.",null)
B.c4I=s([B.bzu,B.bJC,B.btr,B.bKf,B.brN,B.bnX,B.bta],t.X)
B.bF8=new A.aE("3","Why a digital copy doesn't fade",B.c4I,null)
B.bx_=new A.b3("Wi-Fi, your phone and digital TV all send digital data. But what leaves the antenna is always a smooth wave. Nothing flies through the air as ones and zeros.")
B.btE=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/analog-vs-digital/fig-07.svg",784,200,"**Figure 7. Only waves travel through the air.** The ones and zeros stay inside the router and the phone.",null)
B.bIV=new A.aS("The sender starts with a steady wave. To send data, it changes the wave a little. It can change how tall the wave is, how fast it wiggles, or its timing.",!1)
B.bvw=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/analog-vs-digital/fig-08.svg",760,344,"**Figure 8. One wave, three ways to change it.** Each wave starts the same. After the dashed line, one thing changes. The receiver watches for those changes and reads them as numbers.",null)
B.bIQ=new A.aS("Wi-Fi changes the height and the timing at the same time. Each small change stands for a few bits. That's how a smooth wave carries your video.",!1)
B.bv0=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/analog-vs-digital/fig-09.svg",784,608,"**Figure 9. Each change in the wave stands for bits.** Here the height alone picks one of four patterns. Real Wi-Fi changes height and timing together, so each change carries more bits.",null)
