B.bxg=new A.b3("About 30 GPS satellites circle the Earth, far higher than the Space Station. Each one carries a clock that almost never drifts.")
B.bte=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/how-gps-works/fig-01.svg",616,214,"**Figure 1. What a satellite sends.** Every GPS satellite sends the same two things, over and over: the time, and where it is. That's all GPS sends.",null)
B.bv7=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/how-gps-works/fig-02.svg",616,372,"**Figure 2. GPS only goes one way.** The satellites talk and your phone listens. If an app shares where you are, that happens later, over Wi-Fi or cell data. You control it in your phone's settings.",null)
B.buN=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/how-gps-works/fig-03.svg",616,236,"**Figure 3. No bars, still a dot.** Your phone only listens, so GPS works with no cell plan and no internet.",null)
B.bsX=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/how-gps-works/fig-04.svg",616,470,"**Figure 4. Everyone, everywhere.** The satellites send to the whole Earth at once, so any phone can listen, for free. Not to scale.",null)
B.c1U=s([B.bxg,B.bte,B.bv7,B.buN,B.bsX],t.X)
B.bD2=new A.aE("2","Satellites that send the time",B.c1U,null)
B.bxq=new A.b3("A radio signal is fast, but it isn't instant. It takes a tiny moment to reach you. Your phone measures that moment. A longer wait means that satellite is farther away.")
B.bsc=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/how-gps-works/fig-05.svg",616,250,"**Figure 5. Farther away, longer wait.** The wait tells your phone how far away each satellite is. Not to scale.",null)
B.bIf=new A.aS("The timing has to be exact. In a billionth of a second, the signal travels about 30 cm (1 foot).",!1)
B.bsK=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/how-gps-works/fig-06.svg",616,280,"**Figure 6. A tiny slip, a big miss.** A clock that is off by a hair puts your dot far from where you stand. Not to scale.",null)
B.brJ=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/how-gps-works/fig-07.svg",616,556,"**Figure 7. Why it takes four.** Each satellite tells your phone how far away it is. Three distances meet at one spot. The fourth satellite corrects your phone's clock, which isn't as good as the ones in space. Drawn flat, not to scale.",null)
B.bIT=new A.aS("Most of the time your phone can hear far more than four. More satellites give a better answer.",!1)
B.bnB=new A.bC(B.ak,"A fun fact","Einstein is part of every map app. Clocks in orbit tick at a slightly different speed than clocks on the ground. GPS corrects for that every day. Without the fix, your dot would slowly wander off.",null,null,B.r)
B.clZ=s([B.bxq,B.bsc,B.bIf,B.bsK,B.brJ,B.bIT,B.bnB],t.X)
B.bEv=new A.aE("3","Four satellites, one spot",B.clZ,null)
B.byQ=new A.b3("GPS needs to see the sky. Anything overhead gets in the way.")
B.bvL=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/how-gps-works/fig-08.svg",616,372,"**Figure 8. Open sky is best.** Tall buildings bounce the signal around. Leaves weaken it. A roof blocks most of it.",null)
B.bIu=new A.aS("On a city street, your phone may see only a strip of sky.",!1)
B.brF=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/how-gps-works/fig-09.svg",616,230,"**Figure 9. A strip of sky.** Looking straight up from a city street, tall buildings hide most of the satellites.",null)
B.bup=new A.am("assets/lesson-figures/how-gps-works/fig-10.svg",616,560,"**Figure 10. Why the dot jumps across the street.** A bounced signal travels farther, so your phone thinks that satellite is farther away than it is. Not to scale.",null)
