B.bkg=new A.ib("In the teaching view the same 16 subcarriers are kept, so in HE they span a quarter of the width and the drawn wave turns more slowly. Real HE fills the same 20 MHz with 4 times as many subcarriers: switch to Real Wi-Fi to see it.",null)
B.bkh=new A.ib("Four times the samples per symbol gives bins a quarter as wide and a symbol four times as long: the same trade as N above. The guard interval is added on top of these times.",null)
B.bki=new A.ib("ENBW is computed from the window itself: N x sum(w\xb2) / (sum w)\xb2, and RBW = ENBW x Fs/N. More samples give finer bins and a longer capture; bin spacing times capture time is always 1.",null)
B.bkj=new A.ib("A catch is any instant the signal was within half an RBW of where the analyzer was looking. The swept analyzer looks at one slice at a time, so a hop that comes and goes while the sweep is elsewhere is never seen.",null)
B.bkk=new A.ib("One line per sine: its position is the frequency, its height is the amplitude in dB (1.00 = 0 dB, 0.10 = -20 dB). Phase changes the shape of the time trace and not these lines.",null)
B.bkm=new A.ib("Sources disagree: some ovens hold one frequency, others sweep tens of MHz while on.",null)
B.bkn=new A.ib("Fingerprints from the Spectrum Analysis signature cards, and what this scene draws.",null)
B.bko=new A.ib("Picks a new random point for each subcarrier, from the same constellations the Modulation Simulator draws.",null)
B.bkp=new A.ib("The number after each window is its published highest sidelobe: how far below the peak its leakage stays.",null)
B.bkq=new A.ib("The carrier frequency never changes: the center stays put and 20 MHz stays 20 MHz wide.",null)
