B.dRa=new A.J4("Latency","How long a round trip to a server takes. Lower is better.","We open a connection to a server on the secure web port (443) and time the handshake, then repeat it 10 times and report the average. We time the connection rather than sending a classic ping, because the iPhone and Mac security model does not let an app send raw pings. A timed connection on port 443 stands in for the ping we cannot send.",null)
B.dR5=new A.J4("Jitter","How much the latency varies from one moment to the next. A steady connection has low jitter. Lots of jitter makes calls and video stutter even when the average latency looks fine.","Across those same 10 round trips, we average how much each one differs from the one before it.",null)
B.dR8=new A.J4("Loss","The share of attempts that failed to get through. Lower is better; zero is ideal.","Of the 10 connection attempts, we report the percentage that failed or timed out. Because we time a connection rather than a raw ping, this is really a connection-failure rate. It tracks closely with packet loss for everyday purposes.",null)
B.dR7=new A.J4("Responsiveness",'How quickly your connection still answers while it is busy. This is the metric that catches "bufferbloat," the lag that shows up only when something is downloading in the background. It is reported in round trips per minute (RPM), and higher is better.',"We start a download and, while it is running and loading the connection, we measure the round-trip time five times. We convert the average loaded round trip into round trips per minute. A connection that stays quick under load scores high; one that bogs down when busy scores low.","This is a simplified, single-stream estimate inspired by Apple's networkQuality tool and the industry RPM work (the IETF responsiveness draft, draft-ietf-ippm-responsiveness). The full method pushes many streams at once across several layers. Ours uses one. Treat the RPM here as a reliable direction, not a lab-grade figure.")
B.dR9=new A.J4("Download","How fast you can pull data down, in megabits per second (Mbps). Higher is better.","We run five real downloads at the same time, each from a different independent public server, and add their results together, because a single stream can undercount a fast connection. Pulling in parallel and summing measures your true total download capacity. The first few seconds, while the connection ramps up, are left out, and a server that comes back far slower than the rest is dropped, so one server having a bad moment does not drag the result down. It all happens inside the same window (about 15 seconds); running the streams in parallel does not make it slower. It is a real transfer, not an estimate.",null)
B.dR6=new A.J4("Upload","How fast you can push data up, in Mbps. Higher is better, and it often matters more than download for video calls, backups, and sharing files.","We send data up to Cloudflare's speed servers in a single stream for up to about 15 seconds, then divide the amount actually sent by the time it took. That is up to roughly 10 MB on a fast connection and less on a slower one; either way we measure what actually went out, not an estimate.",null)
B.bY5=s([B.dRa,B.dR5,B.dR8,B.dR7,B.dR9,B.dR6],A.T("m<J4>"))
