B.dQC=new A.xF("A spectrum analyzer reveals the non-Wi-Fi interferers a Wi-Fi adapter is structurally blind to. That difference is the whole reason spectrum analysis exists in Wi-Fi work.",null)
B.amQ=new A.blI(null)
B.dPy=new A.tE("Two different instruments",null)
B.dSd=new A.tL("A Wi-Fi NIC is a protocol decoder. It tunes to an 802.11 channel, demodulates the frames it can, and reports what it decodes: SSIDs, BSSIDs, channels in use, data rates, and retries. It only perceives energy it can interpret as 802.11. Anything else is lumped into a vague noise bucket, and many adapters do not report it at all.",null)
B.dS6=new A.tL("A spectrum analyzer measures raw RF power versus frequency, no matter the protocol or modulation. It does not care whether the energy is Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, a microwave oven, or a video camera. It shows the energy as it exists in the air.",null)
B.dPE=new A.tE("The load-bearing point",null)
B.dSj=new A.tL("When users report poor performance but a Wi-Fi scanner shows a clean channel, the missing piece is almost always a non-802.11 energy source that only a spectrum analyzer can see.",null)
B.dSo=new A.tL("A subtler distinction is worth keeping. A NIC\u2019s channel utilization tells you the airtime is busy. A spectrum analyzer, and its density / duty-cycle view, tells you how busy a given frequency is and what the energy looks like, which is what lets you identify the source.",null)
