B.bBM=new A.iq("Wi-Fi devices take turns. **On the same channel, only one device can transmit at a time.** Every other device on that channel waits until the air is quiet.",!1,null)
B.bBw=new A.iq("So the time on a channel is shared. If 4 devices all have something to send, each gets about a quarter of the time. With 12, each gets about a twelfth. The access point did not get slower. The line of devices waiting for a turn got longer.",!1,null)
B.bp2=new A.a4K("A word on the words","An **access point** is the box that sends and receives Wi-Fi. A **channel** is the slice of radio frequencies an access point and its devices use. To **transmit** is to send by radio. A device **associates** with an access point when it connects to it. **Airtime** is the time a device spends transmitting.",null)
B.cla=s([B.bBM,B.bBw,B.bp2],t.p)
B.bBT=new A.jj("1","The short answer",B.cla,null,null)
B.dFv=new A.b1b(null)
B.bBs=new A.iq('Think of a pair of walkie-talkies. Only one person can talk at a time. You hold the button, say what you need to say, then say "over" so the other person knows it is their turn.',!1,null)
B.bBH=new A.iq('Wi-Fi works the same way. **On the same channel, only one device can transmit at a time.** Every device listens first and talks only when the air is quiet. When one finishes, the air goes quiet again, and that quiet is the "over": another device can take its turn.',!1,null)
B.bBR=new A.iq("Later lessons show two exceptions, OFDMA and MU\u2011MIMO, where one access point sends to several devices in the same moment. This lesson leaves them out.",!0,null)
B.bXf=s([B.dFv,B.bBs,B.bBH,B.bBR],t.p)
B.bBX=new A.jj("2","Like a walkie-talkie",B.bXf,null,null)
B.ccu=s([B.bBT,B.bBX],t.p)
