B.dQy=new A.xF("Three views answer three different questions. A real diagnosis uses all three: spot it live, fingerprint it in the waterfall, quantify it in density.",null)
B.dPD=new A.tE("Live FFT: power versus frequency",null)
B.dSm=new A.tL("X is frequency, Y is amplitude in dBm, one live trace. Tall peaks are strong signals; the flat bottom is the noise floor. The width of a feature tells you its bandwidth, its position tells you its center frequency. This answers what is transmitting, where, and how strong, right now.",null)
B.dPx=new A.tE("Waterfall / spectrogram: frequency, time, color",null)
B.dSt=new A.tL("One axis is frequency, the other is time (usually scrolling), and color encodes amplitude. The color-to-amplitude mapping is set by the tool\u2019s palette and scale, so always read the legend rather than assuming. A steady horizontal band is a continuous transmitter on a fixed frequency; repeating vertical streaks are periodic bursts; a signal that wanders across frequencies over time is frequency hopping. This is the view that exposes the pattern, which is how you fingerprint an interferer.",null)
B.dPz=new A.tE("Density / duty cycle: how often a frequency is occupied",null)
B.dS5=new A.tL("X is frequency, Y is amplitude, and color or brightness encodes how often each point has been occupied over the accumulation window. A frequency lit brightly across a long window is occupied most of the time, a high duty cycle and a serious problem for Wi-Fi, which needs the channel clear to talk. A faint smear is occasional. This is how you prioritize: a 90 percent duty interferer matters far more than a 2 percent one even when both peak at the same amplitude.",null)
B.cff=s([B.dQy,B.p,B.dPD,B.j,B.dSm,B.p,B.dPx,B.j,B.dSt,B.p,B.dPz,B.j,B.dS5],t.p)
