B.cEJ=new A.id("A preset picks these for you. The presets all carry the same power; the sliders do not hold it, so a Custom wave can be weaker or stronger.",null)
B.cEK=new A.id("These cuts are taken from the rebuilt 3D. The horizontal cut and the front of the vertical cut match the file; the back of the vertical cut is the rebuild's guess, which is where two cuts fall short.",null)
B.cEL=new A.id("Writes this antenna as an MSI file (two 2D cuts), reads it back and rebuilds the 3D from the cuts, the way a planning tool does. The readouts then show how far off the rebuild is.",null)
B.cEM=new A.id("Over every direction within 30 dB of the peak, weighted by the solid angle it covers. The worst miss is usually behind the antenna or between the two cuts.",null)
B.cEN=new A.id("Mounting turns the 3D view only. The 2D cuts stay in the antenna's own frame, the way a datasheet draws them.",null)
B.cEO=new A.id("Made by this app from closed-form models, so the exact 3D is known. No manufacturer file is included.",null)
B.cEQ=new A.id("There is no file picker in this version. Open the .msi, .pln, .adf or .txt file in a text editor, copy all of it and paste it here.",null)
B.cES=new A.id("The gain slider uses the practical rule G = 10 log10(31,000 / (\u03b8 \xd7 \u03c6)) and keeps the beam's shape. The readouts show what this exact shape integrates to, which is not the same number.",null)
B.cET=new A.id("Element pattern times array factor. Watch the nulls between the lobes: at 1 \u03bb spacing a second beam (a grating lobe) appears straight up and down.",null)
B.cEU=new A.id("Multiplies every loss in the file. Above 1 the beams narrow and the edges fall off faster; below 1 the pattern flattens. The gain moves so the total power stays the same. A what-if, not the file.",null)
B.cEV=new A.id("Same dBi scale as the 3D. Dashed ring: 0 dBi.",null)
B.cEW=new A.id("A file with a horizontal cut within 3 dB all around is taken as an omni drawn for the ceiling; any other as drawn for a wall. Some indoor files are drawn turned 90\xb0 or 180\xb0, so check which way the peak points.",null)
